---
import ServicePage from "../../components/ServicePage.astro";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What counts as an emergency?",
    a: `A tree or large limb on a house, car, fence or driveway; a tree blocking the only way in or out; a leaning or split tree that is about to fall; or any tree touching power lines. If anyone is in danger, call 911 first.`,
  },
  {
    q: "How fast will someone respond?",
    a: `Emergency requests are flagged as urgent when we pass them to local professionals. Speed depends on their availability and on conditions, and it is slower after a major storm when everyone needs help at once. We cannot promise an arrival time, so send the request as early as you can.`,
  },
  {
    q: "What do I do about a tree on a power line?",
    a: `Stay away from it and keep others away, and assume every line is live. Call your power company (in most of South Florida, FPL) and 911 if there is immediate danger. Only the utility or specialist crews should work on trees touching lines.`,
  },
  {
    q: "Will insurance cover the removal?",
    a: `Policies usually cover removal when a tree falls on a covered structure such as the house or a fence, and often not when it falls in the yard without damage. Check your policy, take photos before anything is moved, and keep the professional's invoice for the claim.`,
  },
  {
    q: "Who does the emergency work?",
    a: `An independent tree care professional, matched to your request. SF Tree Removal is an online service that passes your request on; we do not perform the work. Agree the price and scope with the professional and ask for proof of license and insurance before work starts.`,
  },
];
const extras = [
  {
    "topper": "Why it is different",
    "title": "How an Emergency Job Is Different",
    "image": "storm-uproot",
    "html": "<p>A fallen tree is rarely a simple removal. The wood may be under tension, resting on a roof or lines, or hiding damage underneath. A professional will usually secure the area first, decide the order in which pieces come off, and often use a crane or lift to take the weight off the structure.</p><p>The goal is to make things safe and stop further damage, such as water coming through a damaged roof. Permanent repairs, and sometimes the full removal of the stump, can follow later.</p>"
  },
  {
    "topper": "After the tree is off",
    "title": "Insurance, Documentation and Next Steps",
    "image": "broken-tree",
    "html": "<p>Before anything is moved, photograph the tree, the damage and the surroundings from several angles. Keep every invoice, including temporary roof cover, because many policies reimburse the cost of protecting the property from further damage.</p><p>Policies usually cover removal when a tree falls on a covered structure and often not when it falls in the yard without damage. Call your insurer early, and ask what they need from the professional who does the work.</p><p>If a permit would normally be required to remove the tree, ask your city how it handles emergency removals. Many allow them when a tree is an imminent danger, but may ask for photos or a report afterward. See our guide to <a href=\"/tree-care-guides/tree-removal-after-hurricane/\">tree removal after a hurricane</a>.</p>"
  },
  {
    "topper": "Prevention",
    "title": "Prepare Before the Next Storm",
    "image": "canopy-climber",
    "html": "<p>Most storm damage starts with a tree that was already weak: dead limbs, a split trunk, decay or lifted roots. A health check and structural pruning before the season on June 1 are cheaper than an emergency in August.</p><p>Read <a href=\"/tree-care-guides/hurricane-tree-preparation/\">how to prepare your trees for hurricane season</a>, and see <a href=\"/tree-trimming/\">tree trimming</a> for what good pruning looks like. University of Florida research shows topping and heavy thinning make trees weaker, not stronger.</p>"
  }
];
---

<ServicePage
  title="Emergency Tree Removal in South Florida | Urgent Help"
  description="Tree on your house, car or driveway? Send an urgent request and we flag it for independent local tree pros in South Florida."
  service={{
    name: "Emergency Tree Removal Requests",
    serviceType: "Emergency tree removal request matching",
    description: "Urgent requests for fallen and hazardous trees passed to independent local tree care professionals in South Florida.",
  }}
  badge="EMERGENCY TREE REMOVAL"
  h1="Emergency Tree Removal in South Florida"
  lead="A tree on your house, car or driveway? Send an urgent request and we flag it for independent local professionals."
  intro={[
    "Storms, heavy rain and saturated soil bring down trees in South Florida every year. When one lands on a roof, a car or a driveway, you need a professional with the right equipment, quickly.",
    "Choose <strong>Emergency Tree Removal</strong> in the <a href=\"/contact-us/\">contact form</a> and describe what happened. We flag the request as urgent and pass it to an independent tree care professional. We are active in the Fort Lauderdale area and adding professionals elsewhere, so outside it we cannot promise a response or an arrival time (<a href=\"/areas/#coverage\">coverage</a>). We do not perform the work ourselves.",
  ]}
  coversTitle="Situations We Flag as Urgent"
  covers={[
    { name: "Tree on a house or roof", note: "Do not go under it. Keep everyone out of the affected rooms and photograph the damage for your insurer before anything is moved." },
    { name: "Tree on a car or driveway", note: "A blocked driveway can trap vehicles and delay emergency access. Describe how the tree is lying and what it blocks." },
    { name: "Hanging or split limbs", note: "Broken limbs caught in the canopy can drop without warning. Keep people and pets clear until a professional has looked at it." },
    { name: "Uprooted or leaning trees", note: "A tree leaning after a storm, with lifted soil at the base, can fall further. Treat it as unstable." },
    { name: "Trees touching power lines", note: "Stay far away, assume the line is live, and call your power company. Do not try to move or cut the tree." },
    { name: "Palms and fences after a storm", note: "Downed palms, fallen fences and debris piles that block access or threaten a neighbor's property." },
  ]}
  detailTitle="What to Do Right After a Tree Falls"
  detail={`<ol>
  <li><strong>Make sure everyone is safe.</strong> If anyone is hurt or in danger, call 911.</li>
  <li><strong>Stay away from power lines</strong> and anything touching them. Call your power company.</li>
  <li><strong>Photograph the damage</strong> from several angles before anything is moved, for your insurance claim.</li>
  <li><strong>Send the urgent request</strong> through the <a href="/contact-us/">contact form</a> with your city, what the tree is on, and your phone number.</li>
  <li><strong>Be careful with door-to-door crews</strong> after a storm. Ask for proof of license and insurance before anyone starts, and get the price and scope in writing.</li>
</ol>
<p>Do not climb the tree or use a chainsaw on a tree that is under tension, on a structure or near lines. Planning how the pieces come down is what makes the job safe. For preparing before the next storm, read our <a href="/tree-care-guides/hurricane-tree-preparation/">hurricane tree checklist</a>.</p>`}
  faqs={faqs}
  image="fallen-on-car"
  introImage="tree-on-house"
  introTitle="Urgent Help After a Fall or Storm"
  extras={extras}
  ctaTitle="Send an Urgent Request"
  ctaText="Choose Emergency Tree Removal in the form and tell us what happened. We flag it as urgent and pass it to local professionals."
/>
